{"name":"Bolero","hex":"#88464a","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bolero","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lively Spanish dance in 3/4 time; also an unrelated slower-tempo dance of Cuban origin, in 2/4 time.","A type of short, buttonless jacket or blouse, open or tied in front and ending at the diaphragm."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dance the bolero."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bolero"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bolero","blocks":[{"text":"Bolero is a music genre which originated in eastern Cuba in the late 19th century as part of the trova tradition. Unrelated to the older Spanish dance of the same name, bolero is characterized by sophisticated lyrics dealing with love. It has been called the \"quintessential Latin American romantic song of the twentieth century\"."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bolero","wikidata":"Q15830404"},{"title":"Bolero (1984 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Bolero is a 1984 American romantic drama film written and directed by John Derek and starring Bo Derek. The film centers on the protagonist Ayre \"Mac\" MacGillvary's sexual awakening and her journey around the world to pursue an ideal first lover who will take her virginity."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/01/Boleroposter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bolero_(1984_film)","wikidata":"Q1754755"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Sanchez%2BBlez.jpg/330px-Sanchez%2BBlez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Sanchez%2BBlez.jpg/330px-Sanchez%2BBlez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pepe Sánchez (guitar, left) and Emiliano Blez (tres) with three singers (standing)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASanchez%2BBle